    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_31541ce9d128a2106bf008551851e4029ab9e3a12c68d5b1e08e463b68e6f08f",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "An approximate functional equation uses reflection once to represent a central value by a conductor-scale smooth coefficient sum, and applying the same reflection again yields no independent saving. In the quadratic-twist mean square, large-sieve cancellation controls that representation while the functional equation reflects a residual moment into the same family, producing a genuinely self-improving exponent inequality.",
